WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:04.050
Charles: J.D., I don't use the word hillbilly,
you know?

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.100
I always talk about country people and that
sort of thing and I've always felt kind of

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.550
shy about using it, and then you used it proudly.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.240
Tell me, what is a hillbilly?

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.679
Vance: Yeah, well, so as my grandma said,
"A hillbilly is something that we can use

00:00:17.679 --> 00:00:20.439
in the family, but if somebody else calls
you a hillbilly then you have to punch them

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:21.439
in the nose."

00:00:21.439 --> 00:00:26.420
And the idea is that it's an insider's term
of endearment and the way that we used it

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:31.089
in our family is one, that you had some connection
to the land of Appalachia or Appalachia, depending

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:34.620
on where you're from, how you say it, that
you really love that land and you love the

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.891
people who live there, and I do think that
there's a sort of country element to it.

00:00:37.891 --> 00:00:40.680
It's not just about Appalachia itself.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:46.500
It's sort of about loving the rural lifestyle
and having some connection to it, either familial

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.500
or otherwise.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.570
Charles: You've had the book out for a while.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.620
You've had all sorts of people do exactly
what I did, tell you about how, oh yeah, they

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.640
came from a similar kind of family and the
rest of it.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:04.280
To what extent do you still see the hillbilly
culture in which you grew up to be distinct

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.030
from other white working class environments
that people have talked to you about?

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.580
Vance: Yeah, so that's a very interesting
question.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.790
I think the answer is that it's still somewhat
distinct, and a lot of what we think of as

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.820
white working class culture, I still think
that what we're really talking about sometimes

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.050
is sort of Scots-Irish or Appalachian rooted
culture.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:26.560
Obviously there's been so much assimilation
that it is sometimes hard to figure out where

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.630
one stops and the other begins.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.799
But there is this interesting argument that
we ought to just call it the United States

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:38.290
of Appalachia because a lot of what we think
of as sort of classically American values

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.399
are really rooted in Appalachia in different
ways.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:42.290
There's a really good book to that effect.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.810
But I think that it's important to sort of
recognize, and one thing I've tired to do

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:51.049
is recognize that a lot of these issues agree
in the broader white working class, but I

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:53.640
do still think that there is a lot of connection
to Appalachia.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:01.590
Charles: Well, you know, in the census responses
the group of people that responds to the question

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:06.630
of "What is your ethnicity, background, your
ethnic background?" the ones who say, "I'm

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:07.630
American" are the Scots-Irish.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.770
Vance: That's exactly right, yeah.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:15.290
Charles: Which in my case, I've always bragged
about my Scots-Irish background and I have

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:22.040
said, "Well, we were violent and we were drunk,
but we made great pioneers."

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:29.890
I didn't know the half of it until I read
your book, in terms of all kinds of stuff

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.610
that goes on in the culture you describe.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.310
Question, to what extent... you're a child
of the 90s, right?

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.310
Vance: That's right.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.190
Charles: You basically were growing up then.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:47.960
As you listen to your parents and your grandparents,
to what extent has the hillbilly culture evolved

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:48.960
since 1960?

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.810
Vance: Yeah, well, I think it's definitely
evolved almost because it's been forced to

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:53.810
evolve, right?

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:58.260
So one of the things my grandparents, I think,
realized after they moved to southern Ohio

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.810
was that a lot of the values that they brought
with them didn't necessarily translate into

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.770
sort of middle class American life.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.880
Charles: No kidding.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.780
Vance: I think because of that, especially
among sort of upwardly mobile folks who have

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.820
some connection to Scots-Irish or Appalachia,
there is definitely an impulse to sort of

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.790
discard your hilllbillyness.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.940
There's recognition that it's maybe something
to be ashamed of or maybe just something that

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:24.940
you don't want to highlight.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.470
I think it's been really interesting, actually,
since the publication of the book, to watch

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:34.200
my cousins who both grew up in very sort of
middle class families, they're both in college,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.950
one is 21, one is 19, and the way that the
book has sort of brought them back in contact

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.070
with their roots, because I mean they knew
my grandma very well and they didn't realize

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:48.590
how much she was a sort of cultural figure
and how much they really are part of that

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.600
culture even though they don't necessarily
see it as something that's broader than themselves.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.320
Charles: Are these relatives of yours who
are the children of the one...is it Milwaukee

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.320
he went to?

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.320
Vance: No.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.140
So Uncle Jimmy is in Texas.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.390
He went from Middletown to Texas to California
and back to Texas.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.490
These are children of Aunt Wee, who is sort
of a very important character in the book.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:15.050
Charles: And to what extent were they aware
of the heritage that they had?

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.680
You said they didn't know your grandmother
was the way she was?

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.680
Vance: Well, no.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.530
They certainly knew the way that she was,
and I don't know that they quite placed it

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.490
in a broader regional and cultural context.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.780
And so one of the really interesting things
that my cousin did, she wrote this really

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.940
beautiful piece about my grandma and about
sort of recognizing where she came from in

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.370
the wake of the book coming out.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:38.670
But she even said she sort of tried to hide
her hillbillyness.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:42.720
She didn't tell other people about these outrageous
family stories, and I didn't either until

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:43.720
I got older.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.610
I started to appreciate that they were maybe
something unique and interesting and not something

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:49.610
to be just ashamed of.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.561
But it was only after I graduated from high
school, you know, as I said in the book, a

00:04:53.561 --> 00:04:58.660
lot of my very good friends heard about these
stories for the first time while reading them

00:04:58.660 --> 00:04:59.660
in the book.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.300
It's not something I really bragged about
until I got older.

